                    "file": "phosphor-power-supply/chassis.cpp",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "Note: lines 35 and 36 work above, but you should be aware of a tricky dependency.\n\nIn C++ the data members in the constructor initialization list are initialized in the order in which they are declared in the class definition.  They are **not** (somewhat surprisingly) initialized in the order in the initialization list.\n\nSo in this case, chassisPathUniqueId is declared before powerSystemInputs in the class definition in chassis.hpp.  So the init list above works the way you want.  But if the order of those two is reversed in the class definition, the powerSystemInputs() constructor call will have an uninitialized chassisPathUniqueId parameter.\n\nThis seems like a tricky potential future bug that could happen.  I wonder if it would be safer to move the initialization of powerSystemInputs into the constructor body.  If that is not feasible, I guess you could put a comment in the header file that chassisPathUniqueId **must** be declared before powerSystemInputs."
                },
                {
                    "file": "phosphor-power-supply/chassis.cpp",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Faisal Awada",
                        "email": "faisal@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "faisal-awada"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm aware of the declaration order if declaring chassisPathUniqueId after powerSystemInputs the code will not compile.\n\nAnyway Added a note in the header"
                },
                {
                    "file": "phosphor-power-supply/chassis.cpp",
                    "line": 39,
